WEBVTT
TITLE Runtime environments: activation records, the two links, displays and parameter modes — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
One call, one frame. Every activation gets its own record.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Four words of bookkeeping come first: return value, return address, the two links.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Then parameters, locals and temporaries, at fixed offsets from the frame pointer.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Sixteen bytes of bookkeeping plus the data, rounded up to the alignment: 24 bytes.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Main calls P, P calls R, R calls Q: four frames in that order.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The stack pointer marks the top; the frame pointer marks the current frame.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Returns pop in the reverse order: Q, then R, then P.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Deepest occupancy is four frames, 96 bytes here. That is the number asked for.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Recursion obeys the same rule: fact(4) pushes four fact frames.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Each frame holds its own n. The n in one frame is not the n in another.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Nothing is multiplied until the base case returns: 1, 1, 2, 6, 24.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
With the caller, five frames are alive. Base case n at most 1 gives n frames; base case 0 gives n plus 1.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Q is written inside P but called from R. Two questions, two answers.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The control link points to R, the caller. It traces execution.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
The access link points to P, the enclosing definition. It traces the text.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Swap them and every non-local read lands in the wrong frame.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A non-local name is a level and an offset. The level says how many links to follow.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
b is local to Q: zero hops, straight from the frame pointer.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
a is declared one level out: one access link, into the frame of P.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
g is two levels out: two links, into Main. Seven hops over the six references here.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
A display replaces the walk with an array indexed by nesting level.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
During Q, entry 0 is Main, entry 1 is P, entry 2 is Q. R lost its slot.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
So the old entry is saved in the frame and restored on return.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
One index for any non-local, against a chain that grows with nesting.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
The parameter slot is where the modes differ. Value copies the argument in.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Reference stores the caller address. Value-result copies in and copies back.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Name passes an expression, re-evaluated at every use.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
One program, four answers: 11, 12, 6, 12.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
The call is p of A of i, with i equal to 1.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Inside, i becomes 2. Under reference the write still lands in A of 1.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Under name the argument is A of i, re-evaluated: the write lands in A of 2.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Value-result agrees with reference here, and disagrees on the scalar program.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Offsets are computed, not chosen: 0, 4, 8, 12, then the data at 16.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Round the total up to the alignment: 20 becomes 24, and the padding belongs to nothing.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Frame size times depth is stack use: 96 bytes per frame gives 87381 frames in 8 MiB.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
87381 frames use 8388576 bytes; the next one would need 8388672 and does not fit.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Five steps: find the declaration, take the level difference, follow that many links, add the offset.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Three shapes are asked: output under a scoping rule, the frame a name resolves to, and the live frame count.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
A scoping answer that used the caller under static scope is wrong at once.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
A frame count that includes returned calls is wrong: only live calls occupy the stack.

